      "raw_response": "REFUTED\n\nAccording to the Physicians' Health Study II, which is a large, long-term trial (NCT00270647) involving 14,641 male physicians aged 50 years or older, neither vitamin E (400mg) nor vitamin C alone had a significant effect on the incidence of prostate cancer or total cancer events. The study found no significant reduction in risk with vitamin E supplementation and no significant effect of vitamin C on total cancer or prostate cancer specifically. Adjusting for adherence or excluding early follow-up years did not alter the results, and stratification by cancer risk factors showed no significant modification of the effect of vitamin E on prostate cancer risk or either agent on total cancer risk. This supports the claim that taking 400mg of \u03b1-tocopheryl acetate (vitamin E) does not prevent prostate cancer.",
